cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "FusionFlux: extrapolation, long-range saturation and physics constraints in confinement scaling"
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Liam
    family-names: Salcedo
    alias: lsalcedo100
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-5039-8147"
repository-code: "https://github.com/lsalcedo100/FusionFlux"
abstract: >-
  Confinement scaling laws are fitted to existing tokamaks and used to
  size ones not yet built, so what matters is accuracy on an unseen
  device. On the 6228-row file distributed as the standard analysis set of
  the ITPA Global H-mode Confinement Database (DB5.2.3; 16 devices),
  cross-validation that holds out discharges gives a random forest 29%
  lower log-RMSE than a refitted power law. Holding out whole devices
  reverses the ranking: the forest is worse than the power law on all 11
  devices with enough data to score. The reversal survives, on most
  devices, with stored energy as the target and, in a diagnostic not blind
  to the target, in dimensionless groups carrying less device identity. It
  reproduces on all 7 eligible devices of the stellarator-heliotron
  confinement database, in an analysis fixed before the data were
  downloaded. At an internal size cut matching ITER's 1.82-fold step in
  major radius, with one device supplying 96% of the held-out rows, 90%
  split-conformal intervals calibrated on held-out discharges cover 3% and
  0.3% of those rows for the two tree ensembles at widths that change by
  under 1.5%: width does not diagnose the loss of coverage. Across that
  cut, Connor-Taylor constraints on the exponents and a Gaussian process
  with a linear kernel term both reduce the error, and an ablation that
  varies only the long-range mean is consistent with loss of a continuing
  trend as one contributor, beyond flexibility alone. Four reporting
  checks follow, three without extra fitting.
  Includes a rank-deficiency audit of the design matrix, a from-scratch
  least-squares implementation (Cholesky, QR, SVD), a refit of the
  IPB98(y,2) exponents decomposed along the singular directions of the data,
  and a domain-agnostic module packaging the audit for other scaling-law
  datasets.
keywords:
  - fusion
  - tokamak
  - plasma physics
  - energy confinement time
  - scaling law
  - machine learning
  - extrapolation
  - out-of-distribution generalization
  - Gaussian processes
  - allometry
  - model selection
